What this solution actually does: it is a TCP gateway to two security back-ends. The same executable is installed four times (HSM, RSA_ADMIN, RSA_AUTH, RSA_SYNC); at start-up each instance reads its TOMI parameters, registers itself and its port in TOMI, and listens on 0.0.0.0:<port>. A client opens a connection, sends a 6-digit length plus a |-delimited payload (RSA|autentica|user|tokenSn|code, HSM|valida|hash|clave, RSA|admin|reposicion_token|...), and gets back a 6-digit length plus a status digit (or key/value text). For HSM the connector builds a Thales-style 0905/0904 command that embeds the configured LMK17 key blob, sends it over a raw TCP socket, waits for the HSM to close the connection and interprets the reply (0 valid / 1 invalid / 9 error; or 0+generated key). For RSA it calls the RSA Authentication Manager SOAP API through a generated WCF proxy: autentica calls doAuthentication and then reads token/user information to confirm the token belongs to the user; sincroniza1/2 check a token and call setResync; the admin operations first authenticate the operator and then run a fixed sequence of SOAP calls (create user, link token, disable token, unlink, delete user ...) with the user ID and token serial number encrypted by 3DES-ECB under a key stored in the RSA_CADENA parameter. Requests are processed strictly one connection at a time. After every request the router marks the execution as terminated in TOMI. A RSA_ECO parameter set to 1 makes the RSA path answer "OK" without calling RSA (unless the text 000000 appears in the request).
