private void ConnectCallback(IAsyncResult ar)
{
    try { var client = (Socket)ar.AsyncState; client.EndConnect(ar); ...; _connectDone.Set(); }
    catch (Exception ex) { LogError($"ConnectCallback: {ex.Message}"); _connectDone.Set(); throw; }    // rethrown on an I/O thread-pool thread
}
BeginConnect/BeginSend/BeginReceive invoke these callbacks on I/O completion threads. In .NET Framework 4.x an exception that escapes such a callback is unhandled and ends the process (an AppDomain.UnhandledException handler cannot cancel it, and the host registers none). Triggers: the HSM refusing or not answering the connection (EndConnect throws SocketException), a reset during send/receive, and the late EndReceive callback of a connection that the finally block already shut down after a timeout (ObjectDisposedException rethrown). The main thread also sees the failure, but the process is terminated concurrently. The effect is that an HSM outage restarts the HSM service instance (SCM recovery settings permitting) and aborts every request in flight, instead of returning 9. Separately, Inicializar() performs connect/send/receive inside the constructor (:50) so a failed construction never reaches using/Dispose, and throw new Exception(ex.Message) replaces the original exception with a plain Exception (type and stack lost) - code_review.md section 5.
