//if (!ClientePermitido(clienteOrigen)) { ...; cliente.Close(); return; }               // :290-296 disabled
private bool ClientePermitido(string direccionCliente) { ... IPAddress.TryParse(direccionCliente, out _) ... ($",{ListaDirecciones},").Contains($",{direccionCliente},"); }   // :486
Any host that can reach the port can send HSM|valida|..., HSM|genera|..., RSA|autentica|... and every RSA|admin|... operation (create/transfer/delete users, enable tokens), in clear text on the wire (no TLS, no message authentication), with no rate limiting or lock-out of repeated failures (code_review.md section 9: lockout/rate-limit on authentication endpoints - Major). The latest commit message shows the allow-list was being adjusted, but the call is disabled and CONECTOR_CLIENTES is loaded and never used. Even when re-enabled the helper would need rework: it is handed RemoteEndPoint.ToString() (ip:port) instead of the IP address and compares the raw string with no trimming. With CHR-FR-4 (any instance serves any type) the per-instance ports are the only segmentation and nothing enforces who may use them. Recommended: restore a fail-closed allow-list using the address of the remote endpoint, bind to the configured interface, reject before reading any bytes and log the rejection; add a per-client failed-authentication limit; require TLS (SslStream) or an equivalent network control (segmented VLAN/IPsec) - an architecture decision for the owners.
