CHR-FR-26 - A listener start-up failure is never observed, so the service can show "Running" without listening; the accept loop can spin [Major] Locations: ServiceHost.cs:77-90, 194, 212-262.
Code block (csharp)
_listenerTask = TcpListenerStartAsync(int.Parse(_configuracionBase.PuertoTCP), ct);     // :194 returned task is stored, never awaited
...
_tcpListener = new TcpListener(IPAddress.Any, puerto); _tcpListener.Start();             // :214-215 may throw (port in use, access denied)
...
catch (Exception ex) { Log($"ERROR|TcpListenerStartAsync|{ex}"); }  ...  if (client == null) { continue; }    // :235-243 no delay
ParametrizacionesAsync finishes successfully as soon as the listener task exists, so OnStart's fault continuation (ExitCode = 1; Stop();, :77-90) never runs; the Start() exception sits unobserved in _listenerTask (only OnStop ever looks at it, and only with WaitAny). The result is a service that the SCM reports as Running, that TOMI believes owns its port (MarcarPuertoEnUsoAsync ran earlier), and that accepts nothing. Separately, a persistent non-cancellation accept error (for example ObjectDisposedException after the listener is stopped) goes to the generic catch and continues without delay, writing an ERROR line per iteration; a normal shutdown also logs SocketException as an ERROR (:230-234). Recommended: observe the listener task and stop the service on failure; back off on repeated accept errors.
Code block (csharp)
_listenerTask = TcpListenerStartAsync(port, ct);
_listenerTask.ContinueWith(t => { Log($"ERROR|Listener|{t.Exception}"); ExitCode = 1; Stop(); }, TaskContinuationOptions.OnlyOnFaulted);
...
catch (Exception ex) { Log(...); await Task.Delay(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(1), token); }
Benefit: a busy or forbidden port stops the service loudly (event log/SCM recovery) instead of leaving a silent dead endpoint. Suggested JIRA: CHR-26 (Major).
