Benefit: predictable stop within the SCM time-out; no hang when the setting differs. Suggested JIRA: CHR-27 (Major).
CHR-FR-28 - Requests, replies and the HSM exchange are converted with `Encoding.ASCII`, which silently replaces non-ASCII characters [Major, verify] Locations: ServiceHost.cs:302, 306, 396, 399; Utilidades\AsynchronousClient.cs:152, 201; (dead) SocketHelper.cs.
Any byte above 127 decodes as ?. The admin operations carry first and last names (altausuario_token, traspaso_token) for a Mexican user base - Peña, Núñez, Ángel - and these are written to RSA as Pe?a or Pe??a (UTF-8 two-byte sequences decode as two ?). Because the 6-digit prefix counts bytes, the framing itself stays consistent, which hides the damage; no error is raised. The encoding the callers actually send is unverified. Recommended: agree one explicit encoding with the callers (UTF-8 or Windows-1252), declare it in one constant used by the listener, response writer and HSM client (HSM commands themselves are ASCII), and add a unit test with Ñ/á. Benefit: names reach RSA unchanged; the encoding contract is documented. Suggested JIRA: CHR-28 (Major, verify caller encoding).
