5. Architecture-Fit Note
Workload shape: a continuous, synchronous request/response listener (one short-lived TCP connection per call) in front of two stateless remote systems, with four multi-step orchestrations (the admin flows) that today have no compensation. Per the standing architecture question in the orchestration document (./_ORCHESTRATION.md#6-standing-architecture-question-is-the-current-hosting-architecture-model-right), this is the "modernize in place" case: a Windows Service listener is a reasonable host for a low-volume gateway, and nothing in the code requires a message queue, a saga engine or serverless. What the workload does need is (a) correct concurrency and time-outs in the listener, (b) a typed, tested boundary to HSM and RSA, and (c) for the admin flows, restartable step execution - which is a small state machine, not a platform change. Unknowns that decide whether anything larger is justified: request volume and burst profile, caller list and their retry behavior, availability/latency SLAs for sign-in, whether the HSM keeps connections open, whether RSA offers HTTPS and a non-3DES identifier scheme, and hosting constraints (the four instances share a host today). Sequencing: fix the confidentiality and correctness items first (CHR-FR-1, 8, 9, 10, 11, 32), then the listener and security posture (CHR-FR-12, 24, 25, 26, 15, 16), then duplication and tests (CHR-FR-21, 31), and only then evaluate TLS/ mutual-auth fronting, a single multi-role instance, or moving to a modern worker host. Do not start with a rewrite.
